RetroBoot: a cheaper TCG store path into pages that still hold translated code (target-generic, m68k series).

Classic Mac OS keeps data beside code: stacks, driver globals and heap
blocks share 4 KiB pages with 68k code, so most guest stores during startup
hit a page whose TLB entry carries TLB_NOTDIRTY and take notdirty_write():
58 million times in a Mac OS 8.1 start (41 million into one page holding the
boot code and its stack), 20 million for 7.5.5.  Each call made two atomic
bitmap sets and five bitmap searches although nothing changed.  The page's
dirty bits for every client are now read in one pass
(physical_memory_page_dirty_clients) and, while code remains in the page,
only bits that are still clear are set.  Same dirty bits and TLB state as
before; the code-coverage check (tcg-graphics-fast-path) is unchanged.
Lab, x86 rb-lab: Mac OS 8.1 Finder after 10.7 s -> 9.5 s (median of 4).

diff --git a/accel/tcg/cputlb.c b/accel/tcg/cputlb.c
index 20010353ec..84cf2019ff 100644
--- a/accel/tcg/cputlb.c
+++ b/accel/tcg/cputlb.c
@@ -1345,8 +1345,28 @@ static void notdirty_write(CPUState *cpu, vaddr mem_vaddr, unsigned size,
 
     trace_memory_notdirty_write_access(mem_vaddr, ram_addr, size);
 
-    if (!physical_memory_get_dirty_flag(ram_addr, DIRTY_MEMORY_CODE)) {
+    /*
+     * RetroBoot: guests that keep data beside code (classic Mac OS: stacks,
+     * driver globals and heap blocks share pages with code) store into pages
+     * that still hold translated code all the time, and each store comes
+     * here.  Read the page's dirty bits for every client at once, and when
+     * code remains in the page (the entry keeps its notdirty callback) set
+     * only the VGA and migration bits that are still clear, instead of two
+     * atomic read-modify-writes and five more bitmap searches per store.
+     */
+    uint8_t bits = physical_memory_page_dirty_clients(ram_addr);
+
+    if (!(bits & (1 << DIRTY_MEMORY_CODE))) {
         tb_invalidate_phys_range_fast(cpu, ram_addr, size, retaddr);
+        bits = physical_memory_page_dirty_clients(ram_addr);
+        if (!(bits & (1 << DIRTY_MEMORY_CODE))) {
+            uint8_t clean = DIRTY_CLIENTS_NOCODE & ~bits;
+
+            if (clean) {
+                physical_memory_set_dirty_range(ram_addr, size, clean);
+            }
+            return;
+        }
     }
 
     /*
